{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794437834,"nonce":"td4HhMCWyED1aDsyAyAGQsOTab4=","message":"Baseline, 2026-11-11. Medians per frame in ms.\n\nMethod. Desktop: `cargo test -p blinc_recorder --features headless --lib frame_budget -- --ignored --nocapture` on an M1 Pro (Metal), 1280x720, 6 warm-up frames then 12 to 40 timed, three runs that agree within 5% (the machine was loaded, load average about 8). The paint breakdown comes from the renderer's own `blinc_app::frame_timing` trace events. Phone: iPhone 12 Pro Max (A14, 1284x2778), mobile/example release build, one scene per launch chosen by `BLINC_BENCH` (cards, cards_plain, list, spinners, flow), frame statistics from `BLINC_BENCH_STATS`, scripted touches from `BLINC_BENCH_SCROLL` (nothing touched on the phone), warnings-only logging, a 75 s cool-down between launches, steady windows only. GPU time is from a Metal System Trace attached for 6 s. The scenes and hooks are in the working tree, not committed.\n\nDesktop (M1 Pro)\n\n| scene | nodes | input | update | paint | total cpu | path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 200 cards, css transition, with text | 405 | 0 | 1.8 | 47 to 50 | 49 to 51 | full |\n| 200 cards, css transition, no text | 205 | 0 | 1.1 | 22.5 | 23.6 | full |\n| 200 cards, keyframe animation, with text | 405 | 0 | 1.9 | 46.5 | 48.4 | full |\n| 200 cards, keyframe animation, no text | 205 | 0 | 1.1 | 7.0 | 8.0 | cache_blit |\n| list of 2,000 rows, steady scroll | 4007 | 0.7 | 19.3 | 3.9 | 24.0 | full |\n| same, trackpad fling (decaying wheel deltas) | 4007 | 0.7 | 19.3 | 3.8 | 23.9 | full |\n| 3 spinners, idle window | 17 | 0 | 0.17 | 1.7 | 1.85 | full |\n| resize (full rebuild per size) | 405 | 12.1 | 2.5 | 48 | 62 | full |\n| flow shader canvas | 6 | 0 | 0.09 | 0.10 | 0.19 | cache_blit |\n\nPaint splits as walker 0.4, collect 0.2, rest 47 for the text cards, and walker 1.8, collect 0.7, rest 2.4 for the list. The GPU wait over an empty submission is under 0.1 ms in every scene but one, so it cannot be resolved this way; GPU time needs the Metal trace.\n\niPhone 12 Pro Max (A14)\n\n| scene | fps | build | acquire | render | GPU per frame | memory |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3 spinners | 60 | 0.07 | 0.06 | 1.47 | 1.3 render pass; 2.6 all encoders | 130 MB |\n| flow shader | 60 | 0.03 | 14.9 (vsync) | 1.37 | 2.2 all encoders (flow pass 0.9, compositor 1.2) | 109 MB |\n| 200 cards, no text | 36 | 0.37 | 15.0 | 16.2 | not resolved (the trace slows the scene by a third) | 885 MB |\n| 200 cards, with text | killed 2 s after launch, every run | | | | | over the limit |\n| list of 2,000 rows, scripted drag and fling | 9.3 | 44 | 0.02 | 3.4 | 9.2 | 129 MB |\n\nCards with text, by count: 20 cards 40 fps, render 6.9, 490 to 700 MB; 60 cards 30 fps, 17.5, 1.1 to 1.6 GB; 100 cards 19.7 fps, 31, 1.9 to 2.1 GB; 140 cards 14.6 fps, 49, 2.5 GB. Memory grows about 16 to 18 MB per animated card, close to one full-screen RGBA texture (1284x2778x4 = 14.3 MB); the cause is not confirmed. Render grows about 0.35 ms per card.\n\nThings the numbers say\n- The 200 text cards on the phone are killed, and on the desktop they take the full paint path every frame at about 48 ms (against 8 ms for the same cards without text under a keyframe animation, which takes the fast path). That is the animated-subtree-with-text fallback the plan targets, and it is also a memory problem.\n- With no text the phone is GPU-paced at 36 fps with a 15 ms wait for the drawable. In 3 s of the trace the app issued about 12,100 `Tight Blit Pass` and 12,300 `PendingWrites` encoders against 36 render passes: roughly one blit and one upload per animated card per frame.\n- A 2,000-row list is CPU-bound on the phone: 44 ms in the build step, 3.4 ms in render, 9 ms on the GPU. On the desktop the equivalent is 19 ms of `update`, of which the hover and focus style pass is 9.1 ms of an idle frame on 4,007 nodes (`apply_stylesheet_state_styles`; the other steps of an idle frame are under 1.5 ms). The desktop runner gates that pass by router state; the web runner calls it on every frame when a stylesheet exists (read, not measured).\n- Even a nearly empty screen costs about 1.1 ms of GPU per frame in the compositor's encoder.\n\nHarness gaps found\n- `Headless::frame` does not tick scroll physics, so a recorder test cannot coast a fling. It does not gate the style pass as the desktop runner does, so its `update` over-states a large idle tree.\n- Not measured: the Pixel, the web, touch-driven flings (the session has no touch input), and the 200 text cards on the phone (killed before a window).","files":null}]}